---
import Base from '$lib/layouts/Base.astro';
import verifyinginference from './verifying-inference.svg';
import m3svelte from './m3svelte.svg';
import abundance from './abundance.svg';
import trigonometry from './trigonometry.svg';
import softui from './softui.svg';
---

<Base title="Notable projects">
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      <em
        >This page only focuses on my most notable projects, the greatest hits, since a list of all
        my projects would be much too lengthy.</em
      >
    </p>
    <h2>
      <img {...verifyinginference} title="Verifying Inference" /> Verifying Inference - September 2026
      - now
    </h2>
    <p>
      I've diverted billions of tokens away from low quality inference by exposing problematic
      providers, like:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/blog/crofaifalse/">CrofAI</a> (action: shut down)</li>
      <li>
        <a href="/blog/verifiableinference/">Fusion Code</a> (action: removed from inference market. although
        I was not the only force here)
      </li>
      <li>more soon</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>
      <img {...m3svelte} title="M3 Svelte" /> December 2022 - now
    </h2>
    <p>
      <a href="https://kendell.dev/m3-svelte/">M3 Svelte</a> is one of the highest quality implementations
      of Google's Material 3. It implements official Google specifications in efficient, clever, and simple
      ways. It often even works without JS. It's popular among both Svelte developers and those porting
      Material 3 to other component frameworks (as a reference).
    </p>
    <h2>
      <img {...abundance} title="Abundance" /> April 2026 - June 2026
    </h2>
    <p>
      From <a href="https://pub-a50359bc057145a29fec81fc5062b2b4.r2.dev/index.html"
        >a list of every website</a
      > to <a href="https://github.com/abndnce/pulsar">a way to send internet over WebRTC</a> (and
      <a href="https://github.com/abndnce/0k">a client built on this</a>) to <a
        href="https://github.com/abndnce/cod">a way to use CodeHS as a computer</a
      >, Abundance's goal is the opposite of censorship: create an abundance of websites.
    </p>
    <h2>
      <img {...trigonometry} title="Trigonometry" /> July 2022 - 2025
    </h2>
    <p>
      The Trigonometry umbrella encompasses all the software I built for school. The most popular
      Trigonometry application was Tangent, a private utility application that garnered more than
      200 users, despite strict identity verification requirements to keep Tangent for local
      students only. The ones before it were experiments in data integration, and the ones after it
      were experiments in syncing and privacy.
    </p>
    <p>
      I've since moved on from Trigonometry - personal agents on personal devices are more effective
      at working with school data. Public Trigonometry code is available <a
        href="https://github.com/trigonometry-archive">here</a
      >.
    </p>
    <h2>
      <img {...softui} title="Soft UI" /> May 2020
    </h2>
    <p>
      I felt a deep sense of awe when I first saw <a
        href="https://github.com/qiz-li/lovelace-soft-ui">qiz-li's lovelace-soft-ui</a
      >, an implementation of Neumorphism in Home Assistant. So I spent a few months contributing to
      his templates and porting the styling to - well two themes at first, a <a
        href="https://github.com/KTibow/lovelace-light-soft-ui-theme">light</a
      > and <a href="https://github.com/KTibow/lovelace-dark-soft-ui-theme">dark</a> one, both with complex
      colors and CSS, but eventually a <a href="https://github.com/KTibow/lovelace-soft-theme"
        >simple and unified</a
      > theme.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .prose {
    align-self: center;
    margin: 1rem;
  }
  h2 {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 1rem;

    > img {
      width: auto;
      height: 1em;
    }
  }
</style>
